{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And he took him to the field of watchers.</b> After he could find no flaw in them at the roots, he returned to being a watcher and gazing at the branches — that is, to come upon the descendants on account of the sin of the calf that they made. And concerning this it was said: ״אֶפֶס קָצֵהוּ תִּרְאֶה וְכֻלּוֹ לֹא תִרְאֶה״, for the fathers are not included in the children. And furthermore, that it was not all of Israel who made the calf but only the refuse of the people — for the tribe of Levi did not consent. And he said ״אֲשֶׁר תִּרְאֶנּוּ מִשָּׁם״, for through the calf they became exposed, as it is written (Exodus 32:25): ״וַיַּרְא מֹשֶׁה אֶת הָעָם כִּי פָרֻעַ הוּא״, which Rashi explains: exposed. And all the nations saw that the name of HaShem had departed from them on account of the calf, and this is ״שְׂדֵה צוֹפִים״ — which is a term of gazing." "And the answer came to him that in this too they erred, for ״לֹא הִבִּיט אָוֶן״ — this is ״בְּיַעֲקֹב״, for the mixed multitude alone was the source of that deed. ״וַיִּשָּׂא מְשָׁלוֹ וַיֹּאמַר וְגוֹ׳ לֹא אִישׁ אֵל וִיכַזֵּב וּבֶן אָדָם וְיִתְנֶחָם״ — he brought him a proof of his error, for behold it is said concerning the matter of the calf (Exodus 32:14): ״וַיִּנָּחֶם ה׳ עַל הָרָעָה אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר לַעֲשׂוֹת לְעַמּוֹ״ — and is there such a thing as regret before Him, blessed be He? Is He a man, that He should regret? Rather, certainly even from the outset it never entered His mind, blessed be He, to destroy them, for they did not sin against Him; rather the mixed multitude was the principal cause of that deed which they performed through sorcery and magic. And HaShem thought lest Israel too would stray after them, and He would exaggerate to them with talk of destruction — for a person who threatens does more than he actually carries out — therefore it says ״אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר לַעֲשׂוֹת״, and it does not say ״אֲשֶׁר בִּקֵּשׁ לַעֲשׂוֹת״; rather, He never sought it, but He spoke of doing it by way of exaggeration alone, and not in order to actually do it. And this was specifically ״לְעַמּוֹ״, that is, the children of Israel; but concerning the mixed multitude He did not relent at all, and those who forsake HaShem shall perish through the punishments explained in parashat Ki Tissa, as Rashi explained: they were judged there with three deaths, etc. And to us it appears as though HaShem relented concerning this, and ״אֵל ה׳ הוּא הַיּוֹדֵעַ״ (Joshua 22:22) — that even from the outset such was His mind, blessed be He. But if Israel had been intentional in the matter, then He would surely have decreed an actual punishment upon them, and there would have been an actual relenting — but this is not so, for He is ״לֹא בֶּן אָדָם וְיִתְנֶחָם״; rather ״הַהוּא אָמַר וְלֹא יַעֲשֶׂה״ — there was nothing there but speech and utterance alone, by way of exaggeration, and not in order to do and to fulfill. This is what it says: ״וְלֹא יַעֲשֶׂה״, ״וְלֹא יְקִימֶנָּה״." "And the reason for the doubling in this verse is that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: ״הַנִּיחָה לִּי וְיִחַר אַפִּי בָהֶם וַאֲכַלֵּם״ etc. (Exodus 32:10) — behold, there was a gentle utterance and a good promise to Moses there, for He said to him: ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״, and there was a harsh utterance concerning Israel — and neither of them was fulfilled. Corresponding to the good that He said He would do for Moses, it is said here: ״לֹא אִישׁ אֵל וִיכַזֵּב״, for the language of lying indicates one who promises to do good and does not fulfill. And corresponding to that, it says: ״הַהוּא אָמַר״ — a gentle utterance — ״וְלֹא יַעֲשֶׂה״. And corresponding to the evil, it says: ״וּבֶן אָדָם וְיִתְנֶחָם״, for the language of relenting applies to evil. And corresponding to this it says: ״וְדִבֶּר וְלֹא יְקִימֶנָּה״ — in the manner explained." "And he said: <b>Behold, to bless I have taken.</b> That is, for myself — for I have already said ״וּתְהִי אַחֲרִיתִי כָּמֹהוּ״, and if I curse them, I curse myself as well; therefore ״לֹא אֲשִׁיבֶנָּה״. ״לֹא הִבִּיט אָוֶן בְּיַעֲקֹב״ — You took me to the field of watchers to be a watcher and to gaze upon their iniquity and trouble; but how can I gaze if HaShem did not gaze upon their trouble and iniquity? And even those sinners did not deny Elohim entirely, but rather joined the name of Heaven to something else — for thus it says ״אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלוּךָ״ with a vav, and our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Sanhedrin 63a): were it not for the vav in ״הֶעֱלוּךָ״, the enemies of Israel would have been liable to utter destruction, for the vav teaches that they did not direct their prayer to Elohim alone but rather acknowledged that HaShem is Elohim, and that this calf was appointed as king over all the earth by decree of the Divine Power — therefore they said ״אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלוּךָ״. And this is what it says here: ״ה׳ אֱלֹהָיו עִמּוֹ״ — for they claimed that HaShem His Elohim is with him, with the calf, and that He delivered the governance to it to be among them as a king and not an actual deity. This is what it says: ״וּתְרוּעַת מֶלֶךְ בּוֹ״." "Or one may say, that even at the time of the sin — ״ה׳ אֱלֹהָיו עִמּוֹ״: they did not deny Him entirely, and the acclaim of the King of the universe remained upon him. And know that this is so, for behold the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: ״רֵד כִּי שִׁחֵת עַמְּךָ אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלֵיתָ״ (Exodus 32:7) — that is, the people that Moses brought up; and this He surely did not say concerning Israel, for behold ״אֵל מוֹצִיאָם מִמִּצְרַיִם״ — and not Moses brought them out. Rather, it was surely concerning the mixed multitude that He said this, whom Moses brought up on his own initiative. And the proof that this is why Moses said: ״לָמָה ה׳ יֶחֱרֶה אַפְּךָ בְּעַמֶּךָ אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתָ״ etc. (Exodus 32:11) — that is, Israel, whom ״אֵל מוֹצִיאָם מִמִּצְרַיִם״ — is that the language of ״הוֹצֵאתָ״ applies to Israel, who had entered Egypt and departed; but concerning the mixed multitude He said ״כִּי שִׁחֵת עַמְּךָ אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלֵיתָ״, for the language of departure does not apply to them — for who brought them in, that they should depart? And according to the plain sense, it is possible to explain that the Holy One, blessed be He, said ״רֵד כִּי שִׁחֵת עַמְּךָ אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלֵיתָ״ — they were elevated by means of the spoil of Egypt, for from there they became wealthy, and through an abundance of gold they made the calf. Therefore He said ״אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלֵיתָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ — for they were elevated through the wealth of the land of Egypt. But in Moses' prayer there was no place to mention this; therefore he said ״אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתָ״." "<b>For there is no divination in Jacob, etc.</b> You already know that the calf was made by means of sorcery, and since there is no divination and enchantment in Israel, assuredly Israel did not make the calf, but rather the mixed multitude did. And if you would say: if there is no divination and enchantment among them, then by what means would the matter that HaShem spoke be made known to them? — of this he says: ״כָּעֵת יֵאָמֵר לְיַעֲקֹב וּלְיִשְׂרָאֵל מַה פָּעַל אֵל״ — for all occurrences that depend on time and moment are subject to becoming and perishing; 'it shall be said to Jacob,' even to the least of them, and as it is said (I Chronicles 12:32): ״וּמִבְּנֵי יִשָּׂשכָר יוֹדְעֵי בִינָה לַעִתִּים״; and 'to Israel,' that is, to their great ones — 'it shall be said what El has wrought.' For the spiritual things, the Holy One, blessed be He, established them with His hands, as it is written (Exodus 15:17): ״מִקְּדָשׁ ה׳ כּוֹנְנוּ יָדֶיךָ״, and as it is written (Psalms 31:20): ״מָה רַב טוּבְךָ וְגוֹ׳ פָּעַלְתָּ לַחוֹסִים בָּךְ״." "<b>Behold, a people like a lioness rises up.</b> It is known that the principal practice of sorcery is at night, for then shall all the beasts of the forest stir — these are the harmful spirits — and all who engage with them are smitten and afflicted, and in the morning one sees that their faces are gloomy; they have neither form nor beauty, and they are weak in strength, for most of them starve themselves so that the spirit of impurity may rest upon them. But this people — like a lioness it rises up from its sleep, strong and healthy. And this is a wonder-sign that it has no dealings with divination and enchantment; and on the contrary — ״לֹא יִשְׁכַּב עַד יֹאכַל טֶרֶף״, and according to the explanation of Rashi, who says: he recites Shema on his bed and drives away the harmful spirits from upon himself — and if so, assuredly he has no dealings with them at all. And this is the correct interpretation."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {3 "The reading 'עֲשוּ עַל יְדֵי נַחַשׁ וְקֶסֶם' was rendered 'they performed through sorcery and magic' based on the context of the mixed multitude's role in the golden calf; 'נַחַשׁ' here refers to sorcery, not the serpent of Eden.", 5 "I Chronicles normalized from דברי הימים א; the reference וגו׳ in the Psalms citation kept as 'etc.' following the Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:34.933444565Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}